From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 13:40:30 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] meshnet: propagate link admin state across VXLAN links
 (carrier follows the peer)

SKYFORGE-SPECIFIC: platform behaviour; not for upstream.

---
diff --git a/third_party/meshnet/README.md b/third_party/meshnet/README.md
index 76fc85a..7e6c2c3 100644
--- a/third_party/meshnet/README.md
+++ b/third_party/meshnet/README.md
@@ -102,8 +102,18 @@ administratively up. Running `ip link set eth1 down` in one pod therefore shows
   peer node's `meshnetd` (`Remote.SetWireCarrier`), which sets the carrier of
   its TAP with `TUNSETCARRIER` (Linux 5.0 or later). A TAP keeps carrier until
   the peer first reports its state.
-- **vxlan** (different nodes): not supported. The carrier of a vxlan link does
-  not follow its peer. Use the gRPC link type when link state matters.
+- **vxlan** (different nodes): the same `Remote.SetWireCarrier` RPC carries the
+  admin state of each end to the peer node's `meshnetd`. A vxlan end has no
+  wire interface id, so the request names the receiver's pod network namespace,
+  interface and VNI. The receiver sets the carrier of its vxlan interface with
+  protodown (`IFLA_PROTO_DOWN`), which leaves the admin state untouched. The
+  watch reports admin state only, so the carrier it sets does not echo back.
+  `meshnetd` re-sends the state after a restart or when it re-creates a link,
+  and asks the peer to send its own state back. A peer without the vxlan form of
+  the RPC ignores it. The receiver acts only on a link end it has registered
+  itself, with the same VNI. KubeVirt VM ends take no part in either direction:
+  the VM NIC is a bridge port under a renamed link, so the guest's admin state
+  is not visible in the pod and carrier would not reach the guest.
 
 ## Local Demo
 
